  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 226 AM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\n***** HEAT SPREADS EAST INTO THE WEEKEND *****\n\nIn theory, our resident quasi-stationary front, which has served as \na focus for convection in and northeast of the area all week, should \nlift far enough north to keep storms away both tomorrow and Sunday. \nHowever, I have trust issues with the more optimistic models at this \npoint, having seen convective outflows sink further south and fuel \nmore thunderstorms than guidance has suggested on multiple \noccasions. Consequently, I added some slight (15-24%) precipitation \nchances to the forecast tomorrow afternoon-evening northeast of a \nroughly Salem, IN to Richmond, KY line where the HRRR and FV3 fire a \ncouple storms along an outflow boundary in an environment \ncharacterized by 3000+ J/kg MLCAPE and 20-30 kt 0-6km bulk shear. If \nwe have storms, these parameters suggest they will be capable of \nproducing severe weather, with both damaging winds and large hail \nconcerns. Strong clockwise-curved hodographs suggest we'll need to \nkeep one eye on the tornado potential with individual cells, but \nLCLs (1000-1400m) appear to be on the high side for tornadoes and \nthe better shear is between 1 and 3km (vs 0-1km); in addition, the \nhodograph bends back in on itself throughout the rest of the cloud \nlayer (up to around 13kft), suggesting poor ventilation may limit \nupdraft strength and longevity.\n\nThe bigger story this weekend will be heat and humidity, which \noutside of storms will become oppressive at times. Uncertainty in \nthe extent of potential convective activity has precluded a Heat \nAdvisory for Saturday and Sunday at this time, but one will almost \ncertainly be needed for most, if not all, of the county warning area \nboth days as afternoon temperatures reach the mid 90s and dewpoints \nhover in the mid to locally upper 70s - pushing heat indices to 105-\n110. In addition, warm overnight low temperatures in the mid to \nupper 70s will offer little relief to those without proper cooling, \nwhich pushes HeatRisk into the Major Category for most of the area \nboth Saturday and Sunday.\n\n***** LESS HOT, MORE STORMY TO KICK OFF NEXt WORK WEEK *****\n\nA shortwave trough looks to flatten the ridge late Sunday into \nMonday, bringing better (40-70%) chances for storms as early as \nSunday night, and perhaps offering at least limited relief from the \noppressive heat and humidity Monday into Tuesday. WPC Cluster \nanalysis suggests that, by midweek, the highest source of \nuncertainty among long range ensembles (given by PC1 for EOF1) is in \nthe east-west placement of h5 ridging building over the central US \nand hence: whether our area sees (outcome 1) oppressive heat return \nor (outcome 2) remains far enough northeast of the ridge to instead \nsee more seasonable temperatures and waves of \"ridge-riding\" \nthunderstorms. About 50% of models favor each scenario.",
